{"items":[{"tags":["toddler"],"owner":{"account_id":37293153,"reputation":41,"user_id":52214,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fcace2172301d14f8b4a4de3ecb954ee?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Worried mum","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/52214/worried-mum"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":274,"closed_date":1736876556,"answer_count":1,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1736759246,"creation_date":1730938707,"last_edit_date":1731424122,"question_id":44407,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44407/toddler-hitting-baby-sibling","closed_reason":"Duplicate","title":"Toddler hitting baby sibling","body":"<p>I have a 2.5 year old non-verbal son who is not yet diagnosed but most likely has ASD.</p>\n<p>I am having issues with his meltdowns, his listening and comprehension skills, and him hitting, pushing and generally being mean to his 8month old baby sibling. I feel the main issue that sort of rides over all the problems we're having is that he doesn't listen when we try to explain things, or can't understand. If we ever have to say no he melts down. If we ask him to do something he doesn't want to do he melts down. If I'm ever trying to explain anything to him he melts down. So it's like I'm talking to a brick wall. I hit my breaking point when I saw him pushing his sister's face into the floor, this is after constantly pushing her over, aggressively covering her with a rug which scares her, snatching things from her.</p>\n<p>I always try to say, gentle with bay sister. Cuddles only for baby sister but it just doesnt work. I dont think he understands and I have no way of knowing if he does or not.</p>\n<p>Any advice please.</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","communication","tantrums"],"owner":{"account_id":37321539,"reputation":101,"user_id":52243,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7d394fed1c9eb229dd3e076b7a7eed77?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Mariah L","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/52243/mariah-l"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":3506,"answer_count":2,"score":10,"last_activity_date":1731173341,"creation_date":1731006213,"last_edit_date":1731173341,"question_id":44408,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44408/what-to-do-with-a-child-who-is-seeking-attention-negatively-and-now-is-becoming","title":"What to do with a child who is seeking attention negatively and now is becoming agressive towards others?","body":"<p>I'm not a parent but I work in the early education field. We have a child who is very strong willed and is now seeking attention in a negative way. We are at a loss as to what to do and how to handle the situation. These outbursts could be over the smallest thing and turn into a tantrum causing them to hit people, throw and break things.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","sleep"],"owner":{"account_id":9267126,"reputation":141,"user_id":44342,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b6d4bdfec6939b15f35c41030c4c8305?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Lehue","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44342/lehue"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":2504,"accepted_answer_id":44412,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1731091925,"creation_date":1731060795,"question_id":44411,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44411/should-i-worry-because-my-toddler-prefers-sleeping-on-the-floor","title":"Should I worry because my toddler prefers sleeping on the floor?","body":"<p>My 2,5 year old toddler decided she wants to sleep in her own room a week ago. She has a loft bed, the lower part of which is clad in wood as a castle with a mattress in it. The upper part is locked with a door so that she cannot climb up when she is alone in the room. For several nights now, I have noticed that she is moving further and further away from her bed at night. Sometimes she lies on the threshold between the bed and the floor, sometimes she lies completely on the floor in her room. I have already placed an exercise mat in front of her bed, but she also crawls off it at night.</p>\n<p>The floor of her room is covered with laminate and a thin carpet and seems terribly hard and uncomfortable to me, so I wonder if this is an issue worth worrying about. She seems content and wants to go back to her room every night. I'm not worried about her hurting herself as the nursery is child safe. Is this just an oddity of children or should I take action? If so, does it make sense to make it harder for her to get out of bed or does it restrict her too much? She doesn't crawl out of the normal height parents bed when she sleeps there (as she has for the last 2,5 years).</p>\n"},{"tags":["development","communication","pre-teen","emotional-well-being"],"owner":{"account_id":3690277,"reputation":741,"user_id":39807,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/WREQP.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Ben","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39807/ben"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":706,"answer_count":3,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1763166269,"creation_date":1731289202,"question_id":44414,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44414/10-year-old-dealing-with-adhd-or-autism-like-symptoms-and-introducing-management","title":"10 year old dealing with ADHD or Autism-like symptoms and introducing management techniques","body":"<p><strong>I need to lead this with the fact that there is no official diagnosis yet - this is merely initial comparisons made by school counsellors.</strong></p>\n<p>So it has been suggested that my 10 year old son is dealing with a lack of awareness regarding:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>understanding his emotions, and emotional expression - he gets visibly frustrated/angry at things, but claims he is &quot;fine&quot;.</li>\n<li>recognising certain behaviours; he claims he is being bullied &quot;by everyone&quot; for being &quot;weird&quot;, when he has a rather intense energy, especially when his emotions are heightened; has very little to no spacial awareness, often getting very close to others; and acting like a &quot;puppy&quot; with any one he looks up to/treats him nicely (getting close, assuming a happy &quot;begging&quot; stance). This is more common with strangers or acquaintances, rather than close friends or relatives, and primarily adults (perhaps figures of authority?).</li>\n<li>recognising his own contributions to conflicts. For example, he may be frustrated at something, and an unrelated input may frustrate him further, causing him to lash out, and causing a conflict. When the person affected retaliates, that's when there is a problem - he &quot;did nothing&quot;, and they acted out towards him for &quot;no reason&quot;.</li>\n<li>He prefers natural light, (preferring darker light levels) over artificial light, and can get easily overstimulated with light levels, noise and cramped conditions</li>\n<li>Constant pacing when unoccupied, unless interrupted. He again sees nothing wrong with this behaviour, even though this consistently leads to frustration of having nothing to do, and not wanting to do anything, aside from constant screen time (Minecraft or Mario Kart, YouTube, iPad games, etc).</li>\n<li>Impulse control is a big one - constantly stealing snacks and drinks, or to play games or on his iPad, and makes efforts to wake up early (setting his alarm for 5.30) in order to attempt this, or actively hides his actions (usually by hiding the evidence in his room).</li>\n<li>When he has trouble sleeping, he simply turns lights on, regardless of how many times he is told not to do this, and ask for music or grey/white noise (rain sounds or running water) instead, which to relax him and keep him calm, rather than keep him more awake.</li>\n<li>Constant asking for the same things, only remembering when he has been told &quot;later&quot;, and never when he has been told &quot;no&quot;.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Efforts we have made to help or control this (as well as suggestions we have been given):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>fidget toys, and to encourage their use when calm, to help create a sense of calming for when he is agitated</li>\n<li>physical activity, like running around, kicking a ball, jumping on the trampoline, which he often refuses. He is currently enrolled in a martial arts class once a week, which he very much enjoys, but when it's suggested he practice his &quot;set&quot; at home, he refuses as well.</li>\n<li>reading to help calm before bed (has only recently been getting into one book, which has been difficult in the past), but he only does this when he wants to, when he is not in the &quot;bored&quot; mindset.</li>\n<li>limiting screen time; we do currently limit screen time to 1 hour, regardless of type and end all screen time half an hour before bed, but allow more over the weekend, extending it to a few hours depending on what is happening.</li>\n<li>talking him through conflicts and helping him become aware of his own actions, emotions and expressions, however this often causes him to become more upset, leading him to blame and put himself down.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are only new adjustments, so the resistances and responses are understandable, and we have been told that he will struggle with these behaviours throughout his teenage years. We do have another 12 year old who is beginning to enter his teenage phase, already reaching puberty, dealing with peer pressures, his own obsessions and resistance to rules and restrictions, etc., making this new adjustment particularly straining on us as parents.</p>\n<p><strong>Are there any tactics we can employ to help adopt these techniques?</strong></p>\n<p>Additionally, I want to introduce him to the concept of his antics. As an example, I have Epilepsy, and before this diagnosis when I was 25 (roughly 10 years ago), I was unable to understand what was going on - a lack of focus, or an inability to comprehend conversation at times; getting &quot;dizzy spells&quot; and nausea, and lapses in memory. Once I received this diagnosis, these symptoms made sense, making it less frustrating and easier to manage and adjust.</p>\n<p>Introducing him to his (potential) condition may help his understanding, but I am hesitant because this diagnosis is not official (we are waiting on an appointment with a Paediatrician), and the potential of identifying as &quot;Autistic&quot;. Again, as an example, I see my self as &quot;myself; with Epilepsy&quot; - this is not who I am as a person, this is just an aspect of my physiology, in the same way that I am shortsighted - I simply need to manage this; as opposed to &quot;being an Epileptic&quot;, and defining my life through these restrictions.</p>\n<p>I want to ask if I should do this, though that will potentially draw opinions - I am uncertain. But if I do, how should I go about this with a young, impressionable mind?</p>\n"},{"tags":["sleep","parents","rules"],"owner":{"account_id":37459299,"reputation":43,"user_id":52460,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/eb74983f53938e44d8bcb7856c8120dc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Maltese","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/52460/maltese"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":582,"accepted_answer_id":44424,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1731511324,"creation_date":1731401932,"question_id":44417,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44417/my-parents-dont-allow-me-to-put-my-phone-in-my-bedroom-what-should-i-do","title":"My parents don&#39;t allow me to put my phone in my bedroom... What should I do?","body":"<p>I am 23 years old, a fresh graduate, just started my full time job. tbh I don't get why my parents setting up strange rules like &quot;DO NOT place your phone in bedroom when you sleep&quot; I urged them for the reason, they just simply reply that our phone has radiation, bad for health... LOL ridiculous response. How could I persuade them in this issue or should I just simply ignore them and do whatever I want... I am no longer a student anymore...</p>\n"},{"tags":["infant","newborn","breastfeeding","eating"],"owner":{"account_id":3491865,"reputation":139,"user_id":52487,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/87286c53d10908b9046a723d8bf80860?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Eutherpy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/52487/eutherpy"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":606,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1751155636,"creation_date":1731444750,"question_id":44419,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44419/baby-fake-eating","title":"Baby fake eating","body":"<p>So I have a 3 week old, born 7 days before term, 3.5kg on birth, lost almost 400g in the hospital, then returned to 3.5kg in about 10 days. Since then he has gained an additional 200g, which I fear is not enough.</p>\n<p>We are struggling with his eating.\nHe wakes up about every 3 hours, and I first let him breastfeed for about 30 mins (which is a struggle in itself since he just falls asleep at the boob). After that  (because I don't think he eats enough just breastfeeding) I either give him formula or breastmilk that I pumped. That's where the really annoying part begins, because most of the time he just pretends to eat, he will be sucking for 20 mins and when I check the bottle, I see that he has not eaten a single drop. He then eithet falls asleep, or is just nervous and refuses to eat/sleep, which is frustrating both because I worry about his weight gain, bit aldo because most of my pumped milk just goes to the trash.</p>\n<p>Please help if you have any advice.</p>\n"},{"tags":["primary-schooler","adhd","problem-solving-skills"],"owner":{"account_id":482697,"reputation":4291,"user_id":13387,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/efdd0371ee7b983ab3b858ea92653aeb?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"dsollen","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13387/dsollen"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":414,"answer_count":3,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1731930979,"creation_date":1731445702,"question_id":44420,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44420/how-much-should-i-make-adhd-8-year-old-work-to-solve-puzzles-before-i-give-hints","title":"How much should I make ADHD 8 year old work to solve puzzles before I give hints?","body":"<p>My 8 year old, soon to be 9, niece loves lateral thinking puzzles and has been asking me for them regularly.  For context these are puzzles where you are given a strange situation and have to figure out what is happening with only yes or no questions.  They usually involve at least one 'lateral leap', ie realizing something unintuitive or an intentional trick hidden within the puzzle to solve them as well.</p>\n<p>She's liked them ever since she solved her first one years ago - she got lots of hints but considering how young she was then it was pretty impressive she solved it at all so I praised her for it. Just yesterday she was practically shouting in glee that the kid in my puzzle was blind when she finally figured out why the child 'never saw his father again'; but she only got there once I started getting pretty helpful and volunteering extra details I'm not suppose to give to her questions designed to get her to realize her presumption that something bad happened to the father couldn't be right.</p>\n<p>She is pretty good at solving the 'lateral leap', for her age at least.  Her biggest problem is that she wants to leap straight to guessing the answer.  while more complicated puzzles usually require starting with more general questions designed to narrow down the situation and get more detail before one knows enough to have a realistic chance of solving the puzzle.  I've been trying to tell her this and encourage her to start asking more of these generalized questions rather then trying to jump to the solution, with limited success.</p>\n<p>Right now my biggest problem is she gets frustrated and starts asking for hints quickly.  If she can't solve it all in one go she wants a hint to point her in the right direction rather then working towards methodically asking questions to expand her understanding of the situation.  I like how the puzzles engage her problem solving skills, but feel she would get more out of them if I pushed her to work more to try and solve her confusion herself before she get's hints; but I don't want to make the puzzles so frustrating for her that she stops wanting to do them.</p>\n<p>I also have the problem that I can't provide puzzles fast enough to satisfy her.  I'd dare say I'm pretty decent at coming up with these sorts of puzzles, but she goes through them so quickly, thanks partially to all the hints she demands, that I just can't come up with puzzles fast enough to keep up with her demands and I'm starting to struggle with finding new puzzles she hasn't heard yet online to supplement the ones I create at this point.  If we don't slow her down somehow I'm going to reach a point where I simply have no puzzles to offer her soon.</p>\n<p>My biggest problem is that when I delay hints she starts begging for them more and more or just declare she gives up and want's the answer rather then continuing to try to solve the puzzle.  I've tried to explain the steps to get better at solving the puzzles, even giving 'hints' that were basically to ask more questions about a specific subject since that would lead to more information.  But my niece still tends to demand instant gratification and doesn't seem to want to try if she can't guess the answer all at once.</p>\n<p>I should probably mention she is ADHD and by the time we get to puzzles her medication has usually worn off and she's hyperactive, which no doubt contributes to her inpatients.</p>\n<p>So how much work should she have to put in to before she get's help, and how do I keep her engaged and interested when I delay giving her hints?</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","siblings","fighting"],"owner":{"account_id":21138992,"reputation":131,"user_id":52743,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/bde6caf9c977f859a1521aceffd295da?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Heeelp_i&#39;m_a_parent","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/52743/heeelp-im-a-parent"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":514,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1731885162,"creation_date":1731871891,"last_edit_date":1731873087,"question_id":44427,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44427/game-for-better-teen-sibling-relationship","title":"Game for better teen-sibling relationship","body":"<p>So, as many of other parents on this forum, my two teen sons cannot stop fighting and bothering each other. We have 5 children and the 2 boys are my oldest, 15 and 13 years old.\nThey get along perfectly with the other siblings (with a fight here and there, but all in the norm). But these two just cannot get along, they fight from the moment they get home from school through any family activity etc.</p>\n<p>I am sure this sounds familiar to many people on this forum and I am not proposing that it is something unheard of. But as a parent I want to try and improve it.</p>\n<p>I was wondering if there are any board games the two, or the whole family can play that are great for sibling relationship building?</p>\n"},{"tags":["discipline","sex","internet","sex-education","cellphone"],"owner":{"account_id":32339815,"reputation":13,"user_id":53338,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UmTFiRnE.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Hilbert Silva","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/53338/hilbert-silva"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":753,"protected_date":1741358394,"accepted_answer_id":44434,"answer_count":3,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1741350018,"creation_date":1732892671,"question_id":44433,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44433/can-suppressing-sexual-feelings-at-an-early-age-lead-a-person-to-become-asexual","title":"Can suppressing sexual feelings at an early age lead a person to become asexual?","body":"<p>If a parent discovers their teenage boy watching <strong>18+</strong> videos on digital devices,\nthey could take decisive action to mitigate the situation. This includes confiscating\nor restricting access to digital devices, implementing strict usage guidelines, and\npossibly setting up parental controls to prevent future occurrences.</p>\n<p>Can suppressing sexual feelings at a young age potentially impact a person's sexual orientation or lead to asexuality?</p>\n"},{"tags":["school","united-kingdom"],"owner":{"account_id":2021785,"reputation":127,"user_id":53391,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/19e3e8306bdbacabd8b428e04d562b7d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"les_h","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/53391/les-h"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":322,"answer_count":0,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1733083039,"creation_date":1732998922,"last_edit_date":1733083039,"question_id":44435,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44435/who-sets-trans-student-policy-in-english-schools","title":"Who sets trans student policy in English schools?","body":"<p>I am trying to mobilise parents to ensure their children's schools are following best practice on trans students, which is the Brighton and Hove toolkit: <a href=\"https://www.brighton-hove.gov.uk/node/776/trans-inclusion-schools-toolkit-2021\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://www.brighton-hove.gov.uk/node/776/trans-inclusion-schools-toolkit-2021</a></p>\n<p>The Tories created a load of confusion on the way out and Labour don't seem keen to wade in, so I think the best way to impact this is on a local level. Should parents be talking to the head teacher or to the council or who?</p>\n<p>Thanks</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","potty-training"],"owner":{"account_id":12826666,"reputation":353,"user_id":39610,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e009980e68fb08b24c46252f6dc79af9?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"cecilian - MonicaWasMistreated","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39610/cecilian-monicawasmistreated"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":3968,"accepted_answer_id":44439,"answer_count":3,"score":13,"last_activity_date":1733170373,"creation_date":1733065971,"question_id":44436,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44436/regressions-in-potty-training","title":"Regressions in potty-training","body":"<p>This summer we went through potty-training with our daughter (26 months at the time). I had the feeling it was still a bit early for her (she wasn't showing much interest) but decided to do it anyway because it was a moment where I could be close to her and give her a lot of care and attention. She picked up easily enough and in 3-4 weeks she was diaperless. We used to take her out, at the park, in the car for longish trips without issue. She was staying dry during afternoon naps and most nights too.</p>\n<p>Then in September she went back to daycare (she went last year too and never had any issues there) and she had a massive regression. We were expecting a bit of going backwards but it's been massive, she would not hold anything at all and wet herself even every 1/2h. Together with the daycare staff we tried to hold the line and not going back to diapers when she's awake (we had to put the nappies back for the afternoon and night) but in the end it was untenable and back to diapers she went. I still tried to keep her without at home in the afternoon because I felt badly for her to let her lose a competence that way.</p>\n<p>However, a couple of weeks ago I kept her at home for a few days because she had a bad cold and by the end of the week she was back to the level she was at this summer. She would hold the pee for much longer, tell me if she needed to poo or pee and when I took her she would be dry/clean and go in the toilet. In short it seemed like it had &quot;clicked&quot; with her and she was toilet trained again and out of the regression.\nThen she went back to daycare and, in 4 days time, she was regressing again as badly as before. She regularly poos in her pants every day and wets herself multiple times in a few hours span.</p>\n<p>I am at my wits end and I don't know what to do. The daycare seems clearly to be the problem, but she does not show any obvious issue there. She seems to go happily enough, she doesn't cry and when we go and fetch her she is playing happily with the other kids.\nMore context: she is our second child, she is a very good-natured girl, she was always calm, kind and well-adapted (unlike her older brother) even though now she is entering the terrible twos and it shows in many respects.</p>\n<p>Has anybody gone through something similar? Do you have any suggestions?</p>\n"},{"tags":["language"],"owner":{"account_id":38153136,"reputation":41,"user_id":53561,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/394c78f5d5eeb3bbd4aadd13a8bb643b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Nata Hubatschek","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/53561/nata-hubatschek"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":489,"answer_count":2,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1751154926,"creation_date":1733316643,"last_edit_date":1734283583,"question_id":44443,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44443/advise-pls-on-how-to-raise-trilingual-kid","title":"Advise pls on how to raise Trilingual kid","body":"<p>I’m expecting my first baby in January 2025.\nI’m Thai and my husband is German. We both work and live in the UK. We communicate to each other in English.\nI have no clue about the German language and my husband also can’t understand any Thai.</p>\n<p>I have see a lot of people say one parent one language. But when we speak to each other in English is this going to confuse the baby? Or when we go outside meet our friends, when we have to communicate to each other in English as well?\nI know that my baby will learn English eventually when she goes to the nursery or primary school. Is this going to delay her development?\nOr any advise on this please.</p>\n"},{"tags":["discipline","chores"],"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":3070,"accepted_answer_id":44447,"answer_count":4,"score":11,"last_activity_date":1738716337,"creation_date":1733625599,"question_id":44446,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44446/how-do-chores-fit-in-with-positive-discipline","title":"How do chores fit in with positive discipline?","body":"<p>My boys are still young (2.75 and 4.5), and they do a lot of natural helping, so I'm not entirely sure if they're ready for firm, consistent responsibilities yet, but I'm interested to know how best to make this work with positive discipline, and what age is appropriate to get chores started.</p>\n<p>At the moment we don't force them to do any particular thing unless they've made a mess in an obvious, anti-social way. Occasionally they'll help us clean up, occasionally they say no and we clean up, for the most part we don't make a big deal about it unless it's a behavior that needs correcting.</p>\n<p>So with positive discipline I'm interested in understanding what the most appropriate way is to make this non-dramatic approach work when they're older and they refuse to do some of their chores. My wife and I are both agreed that we don't want to tie their chores to an allowance, because cleaning and organizing is just a part of life that they need to learn.</p>\n<p>But that leads to the question of: what exactly do we do in response to the situation where they aren't doing their chores or fulfilling some of their responsibilities? We don't want to punish them in a hurtful way, or force them, but we do want to teach them personal responsibility. So what would be an appropriate way to encourage fulfilling their responsibilities without negative reinforcement?</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","primary-schooler","sleep","development","education"],"owner":{"account_id":38476844,"reputation":21,"user_id":54089,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/616b5fb9f4a101de15c9ebfb2384b9b2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Ami Lee","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/54089/ami-lee"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":956,"answer_count":1,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1734441238,"creation_date":1734276881,"last_edit_date":1734423394,"question_id":44450,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44450/7-year-old-girl-still-acting-like-a-toddler","title":"7 year old girl still acting like a toddler","body":"<p>I am having difficulty with the behaviour of my 7 year old daughter. She does not behave appropriately for her age. Examples of her behaviour include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unable to toilet train, still in nappies</li>\n<li>Problems with sleep - it is very difficult to get her to go to sleep at bedtime</li>\n<li>Unreasonable expectations - she cries and screams to get what she wants</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I have consulted professionals, mainly about the toilet training, but with no success.</p>\n<p>I do not know what the reason is for this behaviour, but they seem similar. Could anyone suggest what might be the best course of action?</p>\n"},{"tags":["personality","aggression"],"migrated_from":{"other_site":{"aliases":["https://cogsci.stackexchange.com"],"styling":{"tag_background_color":"#FFF","tag_foreground_color":"#000","link_color":"#0077CC"},"related_sites":[{"relation":"meta","api_site_parameter":"psychology.meta","site_url":"https://psychology.meta.stackexchange.com","name":"Psychology &amp; Neuroscience Meta Stack Exchange"},{"relation":"chat","site_url":"https://chat.stackexchange.com?tab=site&host=psychology.stackexchange.com","name":"Chat Stack Exchange"}],"markdown_extensions":["MathJax"],"launch_date":1564693434,"open_beta_date":1327529121,"closed_beta_date":1326913200,"site_state":"normal","high_resolution_icon_url":"https://psychology.stackexchange.com/Content/Sites/psychology/Img/apple-touch-icon@2.png","favicon_url":"https://psychology.stackexchange.com/Content/Sites/psychology/Img/favicon.ico","icon_url":"https://psychology.stackexchange.com/Content/Sites/psychology/Img/apple-touch-icon.png","audience":"practitioners, researchers, and students in cognitive science, psychology, neuroscience, and psychiatry","site_url":"https://psychology.stackexchange.com","api_site_parameter":"psychology","logo_url":"https://psychology.stackexchange.com/Content/Sites/psychology/Img/apple-touch-icon.png","name":"Psychology &amp; Neuroscience","site_type":"main_site"},"on_date":1734617817,"question_id":30719},"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"Courtney"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":577,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1744105331,"creation_date":1734610439,"last_edit_date":1744105331,"question_id":44453,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44453/toddler-hitting-while-showing-affection-why","title":"Toddler hitting while showing affection Why?","body":"<p>I‘m here because my two year old son hits me during moments of mutual affection and I can’t find anything about it than one other post on here. Since my son was about 8 months old sometimes when we are both having a sweet affectionate moment like a hug or like looking into each others eyes happily he will start hitting me or when he was younger he would head butt me.</p>\n<p>He is an intuitively kind child who is good at sharing and rarely hits others, it seems like something that is especially reserved for me - his mum. Could it be him feeling intense emotion and not knowing what to do with it? Is it that he knows my love is unconditional so he feels intrigued to test its limits? I'm trying to figure out the why so I can respond accordingly.</p>\n<p>So far ive only had luck with responding as if I know the feeling of love can be overwhelming and telling him I love him too which seems to fulfil something. Other times, I have shown that it hurts me and, it’s almost like he did it as an excuse so he could hug me to make it better so he repeats the negative behaviour and ends it with a hug? Help!</p>\n"},{"tags":["infant","sleep","consistency"],"owner":{"account_id":38622961,"reputation":11,"user_id":54294,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a79073fe93b4f0af21109f07f70845f6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Pachafish","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/54294/pachafish"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":411,"answer_count":2,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1736169824,"creation_date":1734695738,"question_id":44455,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44455/consistency-between-parents-around-bedtime-routine","title":"Consistency between parents around bedtime routine","body":"<p>We have a 10mo who sleeps solidly 11h at night. Almost always cries when going to bed but usually asleep within 5-10min. Quite a happy kid so we reckon the cry is just tired or FOMO. We have a routine, put into cot awake, bum pat and say goodnight.</p>\n<p>Dad sits in a chair in the dark in the far end of room (and she can see him a bit I reckon) and is on his phone, she falls asleep. I disappear into the walk in robe where she can’t see me, she falls asleep. If she can’t fall asleep herself and cries out after 3-4min we bum pat her and say goodnight phrase again. We haven’t needed to do extra pats too often.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>do dad and I need to do the same thing after pats and goodnight ie either the chair or the wardrobe to maintain consistency? Or does it not matter as long as we go to her in the same reassuring way and do the same pats if she needs it?</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>I would like to be able to work on us leaving the room eventually but dad is hesitant as this change has cause the settle to take a longer time and he’s not ready, and he thinks she’s not ready either. How did you know when your baby was ‘ready’ for this transition?</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"tags":["sleep","newborn"],"owner":{"account_id":958948,"reputation":119,"user_id":54456,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/45beee9b157c1cace13d6fdf180af9df?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Dennis Jaheruddin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/54456/dennis-jaheruddin"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":347,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1744081062,"creation_date":1735083105,"question_id":44459,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44459/authorative-opinion-on-swaddling","title":"Authorative opinion on swaddling","body":"<p>In the past when a decision on parenting was needed, I usually looked for an authorative source. For example by following the Dutch Health Service we decided to give 1 shot, but not another.</p>\n<p>However, despite searching I have not found an authorative source with a recommendation on whether to swaddle.</p>\n<p>I am not looking for opinions, or explanations on pros/cons or context of how often it is being done. I am also not looking fot information on how (not) to do it. I am simply looking for guidance from an authorative source. It could be a yes/no or a basic decision tree.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Ideally the source would be a country or regional organization (e.g. WHO,NHS,...) responsible for health advice. A research is always done with a specific scope, so university recommendations would come in at a lower point for me.</p>\n<p>Best would be from The Netherlands, but if not that is fine.</p>\n<p>If there are multiple conflicting guidelines from the most relevant authorities that would be good to know as well.</p>\n"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":285}